{
  /* The run is a hand-authored HTML file and cannot import TypeScript, but
     it needs the SAME five letter-machines the React nameplate uses. This
     config compiles that one module (and the two small things it imports)
     to plain ES modules the run can load with a <script type="module">.

     Compiling rather than copying is the whole point: `nameplateMachines.ts`
     stays the single source, and the run gets a generated artifact. A second
     hand-maintained copy of 1,500 lines is how two nameplates drift apart,
     which is the failure six rounds of porting the scroll engine already
     demonstrated. */
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": false,
    "declaration": false,
    "module": "esnext",
    "target": "es2022",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "outDir": "out/run",
    /* NOT INCREMENTAL, for the same reason tsconfig.head.json and
       tsconfig.archive.json are not — and here it is a correctness matter,
       not a preference.

       Two things were wrong with the cache this turns off. It shipped: tsc
       derives the cache path from `outDir`, so every build dropped a 36 KB
       `tsconfig.run.tsbuildinfo` into the published directory, beside a page
       whose whole argument is that nothing unreachable rides along. And it
       lied: `scripts/archive/build-archive.mjs` assembles the site in a
       scratch root and renames it into place, so the emitted modules are at a
       path that no longer exists by the time the next build runs. tsc read
       its own cache, concluded the outputs were up to date, emitted nothing,
       and build-nameplate.mjs failed with "tsc emitted nothing" on the second
       consecutive build. Caught by running the build twice; the first run
       after any outDir change had always re-emitted, which is why the flip's
       own smoke test passed. */
    "incremental": false,
    "rootDir": "src",
    "jsx": "preserve",
    "skipLibCheck": true
  },
  "include": [
    "src/components/story/nameplateMachines.ts",
    "src/components/thread/geometry.ts",
    "src/lib/data/personal.ts"
  ]
}
